<section class="shell band">
  <p class="eyebrow">Blog &middot; March 2020</p>
  <h1>How to set healthy boundaries</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>A healthy relationship leaves both people room to be themselves and keep their integrity
    intact. Plenty of people end up alongside someone who treats their limits as negotiable and
    their own needs as the ones that matter. If holding a boundary feels almost impossible, that
    is usually learned rather than innate: in a chaotic or insecure household, limits get
    overridden often enough that you stop setting them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Five steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Identify your limits.</strong> You cannot hold a boundary you have not worked
    out. Before anything is said to anyone, get clear on what you will and will not accept, what
    reliably drains or upsets you, and what actually makes you feel good. Vagueness here is what
    turns a boundary into a negotiation later.</li>

    <li><strong>Do not be shy about saying it.</strong> People come from different families and
    read situations differently. An implied boundary is often simply not perceived, and the
    other person is not necessarily ignoring you so much as missing it entirely. Say it plainly,
    directly and out loud. This is uncomfortable and it is still the shortest route.</li>

    <li><strong>Pay attention to your feelings.</strong> Your own reaction is the instrument.
    Notice how you feel during and after an interaction, in a journal if that helps. Resentment
    and anger are the reliable signals that a limit was crossed. When they turn up, restate the
    boundary. If it keeps being disregarded after that, withdrawing from the interaction is a
    legitimate response rather than a failure.</li>

    <li><strong>Make self-care a priority.</strong> Putting your own needs first feels wrong to
    anyone who has spent years meeting everyone else's, and the discomfort is not evidence that
    you are doing something selfish. Give yourself permission to have needs and to meet them.
    Boundaries held out of obligation collapse; boundaries held because you believe you are
    allowed to have them do not.</li>

    <li><strong>Speak with someone.</strong> If boundaries keep collapsing, the obstacle is
    usually not technique. It is a thin sense of your own worth, and that is hard to argue
    yourself out of alone. A therapist can trace where those feelings came from and work on the
    thinking and the behavior attached to them.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

