<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Common questions about boundaries</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Why do I feel guilty every time I set a boundary?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Because guilt is what you were trained to feel. If having needs once made things worse
      at home, the alarm still fires when you have one, regardless of what you now believe. The
      guilt is a signal about your history rather than a verdict on your behavior.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What if someone keeps ignoring my boundary?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Restate it once, plainly. If it is still disregarded after that, the remaining option is
      to change your own exposure rather than their behavior, which may mean less contact or a
      different kind of contact. That is a boundary too.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is a boundary the same thing as an ultimatum?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. An ultimatum tries to control what the other person does. A boundary states what you
      will do, and it works even if they never change. If you find yourself issuing conditions
      and waiting to see whether they comply, that is something else.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
